 Saginaw's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Saginaw has set <strong class=\"sig\">32 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 85 that chance alone would give, and <strong>97 record highs</strong>, close to the 85 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (78). Saginaw's all-time high is 111\u00b0F, on July 13, 1936.","t_chg":"Saginaw has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 114 complete years: Saginaw's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.22\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +0.31), about +2.5\u00b0F since 1912. Saginaw's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.11\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.01 to +0.21), about +1.2\u00b0F since 1912.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 8 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Saginaw has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Saginaw's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.42 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.70), about +4.8 inches since 1912. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 32.1 inches a year. The wettest year was 1928 (43.36 in); the driest, 1934 (17.56 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}